   URL: https://studentlanka.com/2026/05/30/5-devils-advocate-ai-prompts-to-stress-test-your-ideas-and-writing/
   3. The Pre-Mortem Analyst. Instead of waiting for a project or marketing campaign to fail, this framework assumes it already has. Prospective hindsight forces the AI to look backward from an imagined future collapse to locate hidden structural weaknesses.

3. Stress-Test Your Ideas: Your AI-Powered Devil's Advocate Prompt
   URL: https://promptology.in/stress-test-your-ideas-your-ai-powered-devils-advocate-prompt/
   This Devil’s Advocate Challenge & Critique Framework provides the intellectual pressure needed to turn your good ideas into great, resilient, and executable plans. Stop being surprised by failure. Integrate this powerful prompt from Promptology.in into your planning process today.

4. AI Pre-Mortem — Find the Failure Before It Happens · Decidi
   URL: https://decidi.ai/answers/ai-pre-mortem
   A Pre-Mortem Analyst leads a council that starts from "it is a year later and this failed" and works backwards to the most plausible causes — with a Devil’s Advocate pushing past the comfortable answers, a Risk Officer ranking the downside...

5. Why You Should Always Appoint a Devil’s Advocate... | Medium
   URL: https://medium.com/@theandyanderson/why-you-should-always-appoint-a-devils-advocate-and-how-to-do-it-without-breeding-negativity-a89eaf7ab59f
   The Devil’s Advocate Solution (Done Right). Many leaders try to implement devil’s advocacy — and fail spectacularly. The usual missteps?The Premortem Advocate forces honest confrontation with possibilities that most teams ignore until it’s too late, transforming fear into foresight.

  librarian_tool_results: [{'tool': 'search_web', 'args': {'query': 'planning alternatives bias context-loaded solutions framework enumeration', 'max_results': 5}, 'output': "Search results for 'planning alternatives bias context-loaded solutions framework enumeration':\n\n1. Generating policy alternatives for decision making: A process model, behavioural issues, and an experiment - ScienceDirect\n   URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2193943824000062\n   May 9, 2024 - Status quo bias (Samuelson and Zeckhauser 1988) could have an effect, e.g., if the starting point is, e.g., a pre-existing policy which needs to be renewed. Insensitivity to scope (Fischer 1995) could lead one to prefer selecting a high number of elements with small scope rather than a small number of elements with a large scope. Affective decision making (see, e.g., Loewenstein and Lerner 2003) can affect portfolio choices too. In energy policy contexts, people's affective reactions to the names of technologies have been found to impact both choices and information search (Jobin and Siegrist 2018; Jobin et al.\n\n2. Problem-Solving and Decision-Making Frameworks : Essential Tools for UX Design and Product Management\n   URL: https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/problem-solving-decision-making-frameworks-essential-tools-karami\n   September 5, 2023 - They guide you through the process of defining the problem, analyzing the problem, generating potential solutions, choosing and implementing the best solution, and then reviewing the effectiveness of that solution. Examples include Design thinking, 5 Whys, Mind mapping, and Root cause analysis,… · Decision-Making Frameworks on the other hand are designed to help make choices between multiple viable alternatives or guide strategic decisions.\n\n3. Counterfactual Reasoning in Automated Planning\n   URL: https://arxiv.org/html/2605.02603v1\n   May 4, 2026 - Although some research has explored the use of counterfactuals to explain solutions to planning tasks (Krarup et al. 2024), there is currently no unified framework or terminology to describe approaches that allow for changes or deviations from the original input planning task. In this position paper, we survey various works that employ counterfactual reasoning (Pearl 2013) within the context of planning tasks.\n\n4. Problem-solving methods: A leader’s guide to the most effective frameworks | MIT Sloan Executive Education\n   URL: https://executive.mit.edu/blog/problem-solving-methods-a-leaders-guide-to-the-most-effective-frameworks.html\n   June 9, 2026 - Group decision complexity: Six Thinking Hats and SWOT analysis bring structure to collective discussion, improving alignment and reducing bias in judgment. Innovation and ideation: Brainstorming and Design Thinking support creative thinking, ...\n\n5. Beyond conceptual path bias: A unified hierarchical framework for learning path recommendation in online educational systems - ScienceDirect\n   URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0957417426027661\n   1 month ago - In this paper, we tackled the Conceptual Path Bias in LPR by proposing UniSys-LPR, a unified hierarchical framework that hierarchically coordinates macro-level concept planning and micro-level exercise recommendation within a shared recommendation framework.\n", 'success': True}, {'tool': 'search_web', 'args': {'query': 'design space exploration avoiding anchoring bias solution generation', 'max_results': 5}, 'output': "Search results for 'design space exploration avoiding anchoring bias solution generation':\n\n1. 5 Ways to Avoid Anchoring Bias (and How it Affects Us)\n   URL: https://www.trackinghappiness.com/how-to-avoid-anchoring-bias/\n   The anchoring bias can impact our decisions by irrationally weighing pieces of information based on their timing. Here's how to avoid it!This article will outline what the anchoring bias is and how it affects us. We will also discuss 5 tips on how you can deal with the anchoring bias.\n\n2. 16 Anchoring Bias Examples (2026) | Helpful Professor\n   URL: https://helpfulprofessor.com/anchoring-bias-examples/\n   anchoring bias examples and definition, explained below. The anchoring bias, or anchoring heuristic, is when our exposure to an initial piece of information influences our perception of subsequent information. The initial exposure can then affect our decision-making.\n\n3. Anchoring Bias and Adjustment Heuristic in Psychology\n   URL: https://www.simplypsychology.org/what-is-the-anchoring-bias.html\n   Anchoring Bias Example. The Anchoring and Adjustment Heuristic is a mental shortcut used in decision-making where an initial, or “anchor” point is set, and adjustments are made until an acceptable value is reached.\n\n4. How to avoid anchoring bias in UX design | Guilherme... | LinkedIn\n   URL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/guilhermecohendesign_how-your-decisions-are-biased-by-the-first-activity-7303766779682545664-K6EQ\n   That’s anchoring bias in action. In UX design, it can influence everything—from setting default options to interpreting user research. To design better experiences, we must challenge our first ideas and test multiple solutions before taking decisions.\n\n5. Anchoring Bias: (Definition, 4 Examples & How to Avoid It)\n   URL: https://boycewire.com/anchoring-bias-definition-and-examples/\n   The first step to avoiding anchoring bias is to acknowledge it exists. If you have come this far, that’s a good step. By acknowledging that our minds are susceptible to such influences, we are less likely to fall into the bias trap that is set.\n", 'success': True}, {'tool': 'search_web', 'args': {'query': 'requirement analysis forcing function alternative solution generation', 'max_results': 5}, 'output': 'Search results for \'requirement analysis forcing function alternative solution generation\':\n\n1. Problem 10 Solve the given nonhomogeneous O... [FREE SOLUTION]\n   URL: https://www.vaia.com/en-us/textbooks/physics/advanced-engineering-mathematics-9-edition/chapter-2/problem-10-solve-the-given-nonhomogeneous-ode-by-variation-o/\n   This method typically requires the forcing function to be polynomial, exponential, sine or cosine in nature. We create a guess solution with certain coefficients which are then determined by plugging this guess into the original ODE.\n\n2. forcing functions when solving non\n   URL: https://studyx.ai/questions/4lmuklo/forcing-functions-when-solving-non-homogeneous-differential-equation-for-second\n   The general solution is the sum of the homogeneous solution yh and a particular solution yp. Method of Undetermined Coefficients: This method is used to find a particular solution yp when the forcing function f(x) is a polynomial, exponential, sine, cosine, or a combination of these.\n\n3. 2nd Order nonhomogeneous ODE using Undetermined Coefficients\n   URL: https://www.physicsforums.com/threads/2nd-order-nonhomogeneous-ode-using-undetermined-coefficients.328834/\n   The general solution is y = yh + yp. FAQ: 2nd Order nonhomogeneous ODE using Undetermined Coefficients.This non-zero function is known as the forcing function and makes the equation nonhomogeneous. 2. What is the general form of a 2nd order nonhomogeneous ODE?\n\n4. TBC Classic Shadow Resistance Guide: Beat Mother Shahraz in Black...\n   URL: https://www.method.gg/wow-classic/shadow-resistance-guide-for-mother-shahraz-in-black-temple\n   The Ideal Week 1 Shadow Resistance Set. An "Alternate" Solution. How Resistance Works in TBC Classic.An "Alternate" Solution. Should players NOT have Shadow Resistance ready for this encounter, if they are an engineer, then they have a second option.\n\n5. Free PDF Editor: Change & Edit PDF Content Online\n   URL: https://smallpdf.com/edit-pdf\n   Direct PDF text editing requires a Pro subscription, which you can try with a 7-day free trial. Can I get licenses for my whole team? Smallpdf is an ideal solution for teams and businesses that need to edit PDF documents and improve their PDF workflows.\n', 'success': True}, {'tool': 'search_web', 'args': {'query': 'morphological analysis design alternatives systematic enumeration', 'max_results': 5}, 'output': 'Search results for \'morphological analysis design alternatives systematic enumeration\':\n\n1. Morphological analysis (problem-solving) - Wikipedia\n   URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morphological_analysis_(problem-solving)\n   Among others, Zwicky applied morphological analysis to astronomical studies and jet and rocket propulsion systems. As a problem-structuring and problem-solving technique, morphological analysis was designed for multi-dimensional, non-quantifiable problems where causal modelling and simulation do not function well, or at all.\n\n2. Society Optioneering Designing Societal Alternatives Through ...\n   URL: https://spiral.imperial.ac.uk/server/api/core/bitstreams/0eb69044-eaf7-4c8f-95b5-8c94787ada94/content\n   Develops the Society Optioneering framework, Embeds Morphological Analysis within iterative design thinking, delivering urban and landscape diversity design options guided by principles of inclusivity and sustainability, enhancing systematic exploration and evaluation.\n\n3. PDF Morphological Chart - Cornell University\n   URL: https://arl.human.cornell.edu/PAGES_Delft/Morpholigical_Chart-deeper.pdf\n   The starting point of a morphological chart is a well-defined design problem. A function analysis of the product that needs to be designed forms another starting point: the product should be described in terms of function and subfunctions. solutions. b The second evaluation strategy is grouping the parameters in groups of decreasing importance.\n\n4. Parameterized Morphology Design Space - emergentmind.com\n   URL: https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/parameterized-morphology-design-space\n   Parameterized Morphology Design Space is a structured framework that defines the set of possible configurations for complex systems using modular alternatives and compatibility constraints. It enables systematic exploration and optimization through methods like morphological analysis, Pareto efficiency, and combinatorial optimization to identify near-ideal designs. Applications span robotics ...\n\n5. Morphological Analysis Reference-by-Area List\n   URL: https://www.swemorph.com/ref-by-subject.html\n   "The Norris Brothers Ltd. morphological approach to engineering design - an early example of applied morphological analysis." Acta Morphological Generalis, Vol. 3, No. 2.\n', 'success': True}, {'tool': 'search_web', 'args': {'query': 'TRIZ forced constraint alternative solution generation requirements', 'max_results': 5}, 'output': "Search results for 'TRIZ forced constraint alternative solution generation requirements':\n\n1. TRIZ - Wikipedia\n   URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TRIZ\n   TRIZ claims that by studying an individual parameter that is causing a problem (e.g., the mass of an object needs to be reduced), and the other parameters with which it conflicts (e.g., the lower mass would require thinner material, which is more likely to undergo catastrophic failure), solutions can be created.\n\n2. TRIZ Methodology: A Practical Guide to Systematic Innovation\n   URL: https://product-development-engineers.com/2025/10/06/triz-a-guide-to-inventive-problem-solving/\n   TRIZ Methodology: A Practical Guide to Systematic Innovation This is the first in a series of articles about TRIZ. TRIZ (Theory of Inventive Problem Solving) is a problem-solving framework that helps engineers and innovators find creative solutions by systematically resolving contradictions in a design.\n\n3. Route Generation System Using Dynamic Segment Penalty Modes\n   URL: https://eureka.patsnap.com/triz-case/route-generation-penalty-modes\n   By generating alternative routes with different constraint levels, the system prevents the user from being forced into an unsuitable route while maintaining the preference for suitable segments. This preliminary preparation of alternatives resolves the contradiction between safety and flexibility. Inventive Principle:\n\n4. TRIZ: How to Use Systematic Innovation for Process Improvement\n   URL: https://leanoutsidethebox.com/triz/\n   TRIZ stands for the Theory of Inventive Problem Solving. The goal of TRIZ is simple: use systematic logic to generate innovative ideas.\n\n5. Systematic Innovation and TRIZ: A Structured Approach to Creating ...\n   URL: https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-981-92-0321-5_22\n   TRIZ is a scientific approach that provides engineers with principles and strategies able to find inventive solutions in a structured way, rather than relying on chance. This is particularly evident in industrial manufacturing, where relying only on experience can lead to oversimplifications, missed alternatives, and underestimated risks.\n", 'success': True}, {'tool': 'search_web', 'args': {'query': 'requirement planning multi-path exploration avoiding solution anchoring', 'max_results': 5}, 'output': "Search results for 'requirement planning multi-path exploration avoiding solution anchoring':\n\n1. Structural Induced Exploration for Balanced and Scalable Multi-Robot ...\n   URL: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.21654\n   Existing methodologies for addressing multi-robot path planning can be broadly categorized into three approaches: (1) exact methods, which guarantee optimality but become computationally prohibitive for large problem instances; (2) heuristic approaches, which provide reasonable solutions with limited computational resources; and (3 ...\n\n2. (PDF) Multi-path exploration guided by taint and ... - ResearchGate\n   URL: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/373112888_Multi-path_exploration_guided_by_taint_and_probability_against_evasive_malware\n   Antitoxin utilizes multi-path exploration guided by taint analysis and probability calculations to effectively dismantle evasive techniques.\n\n3. Exploration-RRT: A multi-objective Path Planning and Exploration ...\n   URL: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.03724\n   This article proposes a novel solution to the combined path planning and exploration problem, based on a minimum-cost formulation problem with pseudo-random generated goals combined with multi-path planning and evaluation.\n\n4. Multi-path exploration guided by taint and probability against evasive ...\n   URL: https://sands.edpsciences.org/articles/sands/full_html/2023/01/sands20230021/sands20230021.html\n   To accelerate the multi-path exploration, we introduce a path-planning strategy to explore the most evasion-related paths, which effectively explores logic bombs and unsupported evasive techniques with the guidance of taint analysis and probability calculation.\n\n5. A Comprehensive Study of Recent Path-Planning Techniques in ... - MDPI\n   URL: https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/24/24/8089\n   This paper presents a comprehensive review of path planning in dynamic environments. This review covers the entire process, starting from obstacle detection techniques, through path-planning strategies, and also extending to formation control and communication styles. The review discusses the key trends, challenges, and gaps in current methods to emphasize the need for more efficient and ...\n", 'success': True}, {'tool': 'search_web', 'args': {'query': '"design space exploration" "alternative generation" requirements engineering', 'max_results': 5}, 'output': 'Search results for \'"design space exploration" "alternative generation" requirements engineering\':\n\n1. Interactive visualization for parametric design space exploration\n   URL: https://summit.sfu.ca/item/35483\n   Interactive Arts and Technology Theses. Interactive visualization for parametric design space exploration.The outcome of this process results in the generation of multiple alternative designs.\n\n2. Parametric spatial models for energy analysis in the early design stages\n   URL: https://www.academia.edu/126043911/Parametric_spatial_models_for_energy_analysis_in_the_early_design_stages\n   2011. Design Space Exploration in Parametric Systems: Analyzing effects of goal specificity and method specificity on design solutions. ACM Creativity & Cognition, Atlanta, Georgia, November 3-6, 2011. 249-258. References AISH, R. AND PRATAP, A. 2013.\n\n3. Observing Architectural Engineering Graduate Students\' Design...\n   URL: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/372251348_Observing_Architectural_Engineering_Graduate_Students\'_Design_Optimization_Behaviors_Using_Eye-Tracking_Methods\n   Computational exploration of the structural design space. Thesis. Full-text available.This paper addresses the need to consider both quantitative performance goals and qualitative requirements in conceptual design.\n\n4. Design space exploration for flexibility assessment and ...\n   URL: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11081-021-09614-2\n   Mar 18, 2021 · In integrated industrial building design (IIBD), incorporating manufacturing and building disciplines simultaneously, design teams are faced with the choice of multiple conflicting criteria and complex design decisions, opening up a huge design space.\n\n5. Early Design Space Exploration with Model-Based System ... - MDPI\n   URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2079-8954/6/4/45\n   Dec 17, 2018 · Solution design incorporates idea generation, alternative generation and improvement, and a cost analysis. This process produces candidate solutions, which analysts study to help the decision makers select a solution to implement.\n', 'success': True}, {'tool': 'search_web', 'args': {'query': 'QFD quality function deployment alternative solution matrix', 'max_results': 5}, 'output': 'Search results for \'QFD quality function deployment alternative solution matrix\':\n\n1. House of Quality | Umbrex | Independent Management Consultants\n   URL: https://umbrex.com/resources/frameworks/process-improvement-frameworks/house-of-quality/\n   QFD, or quality function deployment, is the broader method for deploying customer requirements through design and operations. House of Quality is the first and most widely used matrix within that method.\n\n2. (PDF) Nonlinear quality function deployment: An experimental analysis\n   URL: https://www.academia.edu/74368853/Nonlinear_quality_function_deployment_An_experimental_analysis\n   Quality Function Deployment (QFD) is a common model to frontload engineering design activities with, linking the characteristics of a product vs. the voice of the customer using linear relationships. This approximation is often claimed to be.\n\n3. Heater lamp product design as hypothermia prevention using qfd...\n   URL: https://ojs.unimal.ac.id/jreece/article/download/6630/3346\n   This research uses the QFD (Quality Function Deployment) method in processing the results of the consumer’s voice, design the product concept, screening and selecting concept and analysis the final product design. Based on the data.\n\n4. Full text of "ERIC EJ1098533: Towards Total Quality Management in..."\n   URL: https://archive.org/stream/ERIC_EJ1098533/ERIC_EJ1098533_djvu.txt\n   Quality Function Deployment (QFD). This approach involves management by objectives, It studies the relationship between the variable of learning outputs and quality management, that takes into consideration the needs of local, regional and international labor market.\n\n5. House of Quality | House of Quality Matrix Software | House of Quality\n   URL: https://www.conceptdraw.com/examples/engineering-house-of-quality\n   house of quality, qfd, quality function deployment, quality management, total quality management, iso 9001, quality control House of Quality.The House of Quality solution for ConceptDraw PRO is a tool for designing HOQ diagrams.\n', 'success': True}, {'tool': 'search_web', 'args': {'query': "Devil's Advocate pre-mortem forced alternative analysis planning", 'max_results': 5}, 'output': 'Search results for \'Devil\'s Advocate pre-mortem forced alternative analysis planning\':\n\n1. Pre-Mortem — Strategy Made Simple\n   URL: https://strategymadesimple.ca/the-communications-plan/pre-mortem\n   Pre-Mortem. Uncategorized. Jan 25. Written By Matthew Rempel.Use a combination of internal and external forces that lead to failure. Now go over each of these scenarios. Put away the devil’s advocate perspective and start looking at it opportunistically.\n\n2. 5 Devil’s Advocate AI Prompts to Stress-Test Your Ideas and Writing...\n   URL: https://studentlanka.com/2026/05/30/5-devils-advocate-ai-prompts-to-stress-test-your-ideas-and-writing/\n   3. The Pre-Mortem Analyst. Instead of waiting for a project or marketing campaign to fail, this framework assumes it already has. Prospective hindsight forces the AI to look backward from an imagined future collapse to locate hidden structural weaknesses.\n\n3. Stress-Test Your Ideas: Your AI-Powered Devil\'s Advocate Prompt\n   URL: https://promptology.in/stress-test-your-ideas-your-ai-powered-devils-advocate-prompt/\n   This Devil’s Advocate Challenge & Critique Framework provides the intellectual pressure needed to turn your good ideas into great, resilient, and executable plans. Stop being surprised by failure. Integrate this powerful prompt from Promptology.in into your planning process today.\n\n4. AI Pre-Mortem — Find the Failure Before It Happens · Decidi\n   URL: https://decidi.ai/answers/ai-pre-mortem\n   A Pre-Mortem Analyst leads a council that starts from "it is a year later and this failed" and works backwards to the most plausible causes — with a Devil’s Advocate pushing past the comfortable answers, a Risk Officer ranking the downside...\n\n5. Why You Should Always Appoint a Devil’s Advocate... | Medium\n   URL: https://medium.com/@theandyanderson/why-you-should-always-appoint-a-devils-advocate-and-how-to-do-it-without-breeding-negativity-a89eaf7ab59f\n   The Devil’s Advocate Solution (Done Right). Many leaders try to implement devil’s advocacy — and fail spectacularly. The usual missteps?The Premortem Advocate forces honest confrontation with possibilities that most teams ignore until it’s too late, transforming fear into foresight.\n', 'success': True}]
  librarian_finding: persona='librarian' candidate="Pre-Mortem Analysis with Devil's Advocate systematically forces exploration of alternative failure modes and solution paths before commitment, preventing context-anchored planning bias." solution_class='external-method' verdict='pursue' precedent='https://decidi.ai/answers/ai-pre-mortem' is_this_a_graph='No. Pre-Mortem is a structured planning ritual, not a graph topology. It produces decision artifacts and alternative inventories.' effort_risk='Low. Pre-Mortem is a lightweight facilitation pattern requiring 1–2 hours per planning cycle. Risk is minimal; it surfaces hidden assumptions without implementation cost.' rationale="Witnessed incidents show shell-context and hook-context anchoring prevented OS-permissions and framework alternatives from surfacing. Pre-Mortem's backward-from-failure stance and Devil's Advocate role force enumeration of solution classes orthogonal to author context."
  findings: [{'persona': 'os-infra-primitivist', 'candidate': 'Use OS file-system watch hooks (CAP-222 macOS File-Hook Example) to trigger graph execution on worktree changes, replacing shell-diff polling with native inotify/FSEvents boundaries.', 'solution_class': 'process-boundary', 'verdict': 'pursue', 'precedent': 'CAP-222, CAP-254 Session Worktree Lifecycle, does_the_platform_already_do_this', 'is_this_a_graph': 'yes', 'effort_risk': 'medium/high. Requires kernel-level hook integration and cross-platform abstraction; eliminates polling overhead but adds OS-specific failure modes.', 'rationale': 'The problem witnesses shell-diff context driving hook solutions; OS permissions and process boundaries were never considered despite existing in the repo. File-system watch primitives (inotify, FSEvents) are already enforced by the kernel and eliminate the 601-line hook cost by delegating to the platform.'}, {'persona': 'data-process-planner', 'candidate': 'Require solution-class classification at FR intake before author context loads, dissolving shell/hook bias before planning begins.', 'solution_class': 'schema-data', 'verdict': 'pursue', 'precedent': 'FR-806-author-sh-brief-preflight.md; ask_before_generate; does_the_platform_already_do_this', 'is_this_a_graph': 'Yes. The intake checklist becomes a decision graph routing to os-permissions, process-boundary, or graph-pipeline before author shell/hook context primes the planner.', 'effort_risk': 'Low. Adds one classification step to FR schema validation gate (CAP-108, CAP-116). Reuses existing preflight pattern from FR-806.', 'rationale': "Witnessed incidents show planner bias follows author's loaded context (shell→shell, hooks→hooks). Intake-time classification closes input shape before planning fires, preventing 601-line hook and 5-round review cycles. Dissolves the problem at schema boundary."}, {'persona': 'yamlgraph-native-planner', 'candidate': 'Invoke research-route graph (FR-890 closed-input alternatives route) to fan out orthogonal planner personas across solution classes: os-permissions, process-boundary, schema-data, graph-pipeline, subtraction, external-method, boundary-enforcement. Deterministic collection of graph shapes feeds map judgement per class, reducing findings into structured alternatives table.', 'solution_class': 'graph-pipeline', 'verdict': 'pursue', 'precedent': 'FR-890, research-route graph, CAP-248 invocation-time tool-slot binding, is_this_a_graph Scripture trap', 'is_this_a_graph': 'research-route: closed-input alternatives route with deterministic collection, fan-out map per persona, and reducer-driven alternatives table. Matches committed shape exactly.', 'effort_risk': 'Low/medium. The graph exists and is proven; binding solution-class personas as tool slots (CAP-249) is the only novel seam. Estimated 2–3 hours to wire planner personas and validate output shape.', 'rationale': 'The problem names the exact failure mode: author context (shell, hooks) narrows solution search before alternatives are explored. research-route is purpose-built to prevent this by running orthogonal planners in parallel and merging their findings deterministically. Avoids the 601-line hook and 5-round review cycle by surfacing all seven solution classes upfront.'}, {'persona': 'subtractionist', 'candidate': 'Delete the requirement to auto-propose solutions from shell/hook context. Instead, mandate explicit solution-class declaration upfront, forcing authors to consider all available frameworks (house, shell, hooks, graph-pipeline) before planning begins.', 'solution_class': 'schema-data', 'verdict': 'pursue', 'precedent': 'FR-806-author-sh-brief-preflight.md; CAP-237 Author Brief Pre-Flight; Scripture: ask_before_generate, does_the_platform_already_do_this', 'is_this_a_graph': 'Yes. Solution-class selection is a routing decision; a graph node can enforce declaration before dispatch to solution-bearing FRs.', 'effort_risk': 'Low. Adds one schema field and one validation gate; no new automation. Reuses existing CAP-237 preflight pattern and FR-806 enforcement model.', 'rationale': 'The 2026-08-25 incident shows context-driven solution selection (shell in diff → shell solution) bypassed OS-permissions class entirely. Forcing explicit declaration at brief intake closes the input-closure gap without adding daemon or review rounds.'}, {'persona': 'librarian', 'candidate': "Pre-Mortem Analysis with Devil's Advocate systematically forces exploration of alternative failure modes and solution paths before commitment, preventing context-anchored planning bias.", 'solution_class': 'external-method', 'verdict': 'pursue', 'precedent': 'https://decidi.ai/answers/ai-pre-mortem', 'is_this_a_graph': 'No. Pre-Mortem is a structured planning ritual, not a graph topology. It produces decision artifacts and alternative inventories.', 'effort_risk': 'Low. Pre-Mortem is a lightweight facilitation pattern requiring 1–2 hours per planning cycle. Risk is minimal; it surfaces hidden assumptions without implementation cost.', 'rationale': "Witnessed incidents show shell-context and hook-context anchoring prevented OS-permissions and framework alternatives from surfacing. Pre-Mortem's backward-from-failure stance and Devil's Advocate role force enumeration of solution classes orthogonal to author context."}]
